cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use XANESNET, please cite it using the metadata in this file."
title: XANESNET
type: software
version: 1.0.0
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
repository-code: "https://github.com/NewcastleRSE/XANESNET"
url: "https://xanesnet.readthedocs.io"
abstract: >-
  In this work, we introduce XANESNET, an open-source, PyTorch-based software
  package for machine learning in spectroscopy. Specifically, the framework
  integrates training, inference, and automated analysis for structure-spectrum
  learning within a plugin-based architecture. Its modular design enables users
  to compare, combine, and extend workflow and analysis components without
  modifying the core codebase, providing a flexible and reusable alternative to
  single-purpose implementations. XANESNET supports both forward prediction from
  atomic structures to spectra and inverse inference from spectra to structures,
  properties, or descriptors. Through PyTorch Lightning, we enable multi-device
  training using Distributed Data Parallel (DDP) across multiple GPUs. A shared
  data pipeline uniformly handles both molecular and periodic systems. Although
  the framework is agnostic to spectroscopic techniques, current modules
  primarily implement atom-centred spectra. We demonstrate its use for learning
  structure-spectrum relationships in X-ray absorption spectroscopy. To our
  knowledge, XANESNET is the first general-purpose machine-learning framework for
  spectroscopy with a central focus on extensibility and reproducibility. This
  facilitates systematic comparisons and evaluations of machine-learning
  approaches, lowering the barrier for researchers to develop and benchmark novel
  methods.
keywords:
  - X-ray spectroscopy
  - X-ray absorption spectroscopy
  - machine learning
  - PyTorch
authors:
